@startuml client_connection_stop_activity
title ClientConnection - user-requested stopping

start
:Stop(): claim stopping for the current connection lifecycle;
if (TrySetStopReason(kUserRequested) succeeds?) then (yes)
    :ProcessStateChange(kStopping);
    note right
        Publish kStopping and invoke its callback
        in the winning Stop() call before cleanup.
        Losing stop requests do not schedule cleanup.
    end note
    if (IsInCallback()?) then (yes)
        :Call SwitchToStopState() directly;
    else (no)
        :Enqueue disconnection_command_ at TimePoint{}, owner = this;
        :Return from Stop() without waiting for cleanup;
        :Engine invokes SwitchToStopState();
    endif
else (no)
    :Return without state change or cleanup;
    note right
        No active lifecycle, or stopping has already been claimed,
        for example by the engine after a communication failure.
        The reason is already non-kNone, or a competing stop path
        won the compare/exchange. Preserve its reason and cleanup.
        This is not an open-descriptor check: Starting can be stopped.
    end note
    stop
endif
stop

legend
    This view records the winning explicit Stop() path.
    On the engine callback thread, cleanup can finish inline.
    Stop() from the ready-state callback can therefore invoke
    stopping and stopped callbacks before the ready callback returns.
    The view does not qualify Stop() during connection establishment.
endlegend

@enduml